  "intro": "The conditional probability of $A$ given $B$ is $P(A\\mid B)=\\dfrac{P(A\\text{ and }B)}{P(B)}$, the chance of $A$ within the cases where $B$ happens. The general multiplication rule rearranges this: $P(A\\text{ and }B)=P(B)\\,P(A\\mid B)$. Events $A$ and $B$ are independent when $P(A\\mid B)=P(A)$ (knowing $B$ does not change $A$'s probability). Two-way tables make conditional probabilities easy by restricting attention to one row or column.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "$P(A\\text{ and }B)=0.2$ and $P(B)=0.5$. Find $P(A\\mid B)$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Use $P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\text{ and }B)}{P(B)}$.",
          "$\\frac{0.2}{0.5}=0.4$."
        ],
        "answer": "$0.4$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "In a group, $40$ like tea, and of those $10$ also like coffee. Find $P(\\text{coffee}\\mid\\text{tea})$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Restrict to tea likers: $40$ people.",
          "Among them $10$ like coffee: $\\frac{10}{40}=0.25$."
        ],
        "answer": "$0.25$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Are $A$ and $B$ independent if $P(A)=0.3$, $P(B)=0.5$, $P(A\\text{ and }B)=0.15$?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Check whether $P(A\\text{ and }B)=P(A)P(B)$.",
          "$P(A)P(B)=0.3\\cdot 0.5=0.15$, which equals the given value.",
          "So the events are independent."
        ],
        "answer": "Yes, independent."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
